{"step_index":83,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-12T06:37:40Z","content":"Think of it like a **digital physical safe** inside your computer. \n\nHere are the 4 main reasons to use it:\n\n---\n\n### 1. You only need to remember ONE password\nMost people have 30+ accounts (Google, GitHub, Bank, Amazon, Netflix, Wi-Fi, etc.).\n* Instead of trying to remember dozens of different passwords, you only remember **one Master Password**.\n* The vault securely holds all the rest.\n\n---\n\n### 2. Stops hackers from breaking into all your accounts\n* **The Danger**: Most people reuse the same password (like `vijay123` or `Pass@2024`) across multiple websites. If a small, insecure website gets hacked, the hackers will immediately try that same password on your **GitHub, Email, and Bank**.\n* **The Solution**: With this app, you can generate a crazy strong, unique password for every site (e.g., `x8#K9$vL!2pQ@mZ5`). If one site gets hacked, **none of your other accounts are at risk**.\n\n---\n\n### 3. 100% Private — Unlike Cloud Password Managers\nPopular cloud services (like LastPass or 1Password) store your data on their remote servers, and companies have been hacked in the past.\n* **This app never connects to the internet.**\n* No company owns your data.\n* No monthly subscription fees.\n* It is encrypted with **AES-256** (bank-grade encryption) and lives strictly inside your browser.\n\n---\n\n### 4. Safe place for other secrets (Cards & Notes)\nBeyond logins, you can store:\n* **Payment Cards**: Card numbers, expiry, CVVs so you don't have to carry your physical wallet to your desk.\n* **Secure Notes**: Crypto recovery phrases, software license keys, Wi-Fi passwords, or PIN codes.\n\n---\n\n### Summary\nWhenever you sign up for a new website or app:\n1. Open the vault and generate a strong password.\n2. Save it here.\n3. Next time you need to log in, just open your vault, click **Copy (📋)**, and paste it!","thinking":"**Determining the Value Proposition**\n\nThe user has created an offline password manager. The core value lies in securing credentials. This is because passwords are often reused and vulnerable. An offline manager encrypts and stores passwords locally, minimizing online exposure and risk of breaches. This is especially critical for accounts like the GitHub entry just stored.\n\n\n"}
